1. What is React Router DOM?

React Router DOM is a library that allows you to navigate between different pages of a React application without reloading the entire website.

Imagine you’re building a website with these pages:

* / → Home page
* /about → About page
* /login → Login page
* /profile → Profile page


BrowserRouter

Uses the browser’s History API (pushState), so URLs look normal:

https://example.com/about
https://example.com/users/42

This is the default choice for most apps. The catch is that the server must be configured to serve index.html for every route. Otherwise, refreshing on /about or opening that link directly returns a 404, because the server looks for an actual /about file. Vite’s dev server handles this for you, but production hosting (Nginx, Netlify, Vercel, etc.) needs a fallback rule.

HashRouter

Puts the route after a # in the URL:

https://example.com/#/about
https://example.com/#/users/42

The browser never sends anything after # to the server, so the server only ever sees /. That means no server configuration is needed and refreshes never 404. Downsides: uglier URLs and weaker SEO. It’s useful for static hosting where you can’t set up fallbacks (e.g., GitHub Pages without workarounds) or for things like Electron apps.

MemoryRouter

Keeps the route history in memory, so the URL in the address bar never changes. Navigation works inside the app, but there’s no link sharing, and refreshing resets it. It’s mainly used for:

Tests (rendering components with routing without a browser URL)
React Native or embedded widgets
Apps that live inside something that doesn’t have a normal URL bar

Usage (all three are used the same way):

javascript
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom'

<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
    <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>